
  - [1m[38;5;153mvalues[0m — a plain object of field name → value, exactly what the submit handler already builds at [38;5;153mapp.js:19-21[39m.
  - [1m[38;5;153mschema[0m — field name → array of rules, e.g. [38;5;153m{ username: ["required"], password: ["required"] }[39m. A rule entry is
    either a string (uses the default message) or [38;5;153m{ rule, message }[39m for a custom one.
  - [1mResult[0m — [38;5;153m{ valid: true, errors: {} }[39m on success; otherwise [38;5;153mvalid: false[39m and [38;5;153merrors[39m keyed by field name, e.g. [38;5;153m{ [39m
    [38;5;153musername: "Username is required" }[39m. Every field is checked, so a form reports all its problems at once; within one
    field the first failing rule wins.

  Semantics worth pinning down now, since they're the interface:

  - [38;5;153mrequired[39m fails on [38;5;153mundefined[39m, [38;5;153mnull[39m, empty string, and whitespace-only. That is slightly stricter than today's
    [38;5;153m!formData.username[39m, which also rejects [38;5;153m0[39m and [38;5;153mfalse[39m — irrelevant for text inputs, but the whitespace case is a real
    improvement for a login field.
  - A schema naming a rule that doesn't exist [1mthrows[0m. That's a programmer error and should fail loudly rather than
    silently passing.
  - A field in the schema but absent from [38;5;153mvalues[39m is treated as empty and fails [38;5;153mrequired[39m. Extra keys in [38;5;153mvalues[39m with no
    schema entry are ignored.

  [1mDual export[0m, per your choice: [38;5;153mmodule.exports = { validate }[39m when [38;5;153mmodule[39m exists, otherwise [38;5;153mglobalThis.Validation = { [39m
  [38;5;153mvalidate }[39m.

  [1mCall-site changes.[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets [38;5;153m<script src="src/validation.js"></script>[39m immediately before the existing [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
  tag at line 13. In [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m is deleted and replaced by a [38;5;153mLOGIN_SCHEMA[39m constant plus a
  [38;5;153mValidation.validate(...)[39m call; the submit handler otherwise stays as it is.

  [1mOne deviation from "behavior unchanged" I want you to see rather than discover.[0m Today a failure logs [38;5;153mValidation error:
[39m  [38;5;153mMissing required fields[39m — one generic string regardless of which field is empty. With per-field errors the console
  line becomes field-specific ([38;5;153mUsername is required[39m). Nothing on the page changes and no user-facing behavior changes,
  but that console text is not byte-identical. The alternative is collapsing the per-field errors back into the old
  generic string, which throws away the main benefit of the new shape. I'd take the field-specific message.

  Does this section look right — particularly the [38;5;153m{ valid, errors }[39m shape, the custom-message escape hatch, and that
  console message change?

